#!/usr/bin/env bash
# fusion-edge-answers — what prior curator runs already asked the user about a
# work-item edge.
#
# Usage:
#   fusion-edge-answers
#
# Takes no arguments. Output on stdout, one KEY=value per line, then one row per
# answered pair:
#
#   anchor=workbench-root
#   run-files=15
#   edge-entries=2
#   pairs=2
#   unreadable=0
#   verdict=answers
#   none        <dependent>.md -> <target>.md  into Cross-references
#
# The row is four fixed columns — the outcome value, the dependent, the target,
# the field the edge would be written into. The outcome column carries one of
# the five values `agents/curator.md` `## The run file` authors — `applied`,
# `skipped`, `not-offered`, `stale`, `failed` — or one of two that are not
# outcome values at all: `none`, the entry carries no outcome row, and
# `unreadable`, its row carries no vocabulary word. Both say no answer was read.
#
# WHAT IT IS FOR. `agents/curator.md` `### The suppression read` must not
# re-propose an edge the user already answered, and the record of those answers
# is every curator run file in the workbench: 975 220 bytes over 15 files on
# fusion's own tree at 2026-09-18, of which exactly ONE carries an edge entry,
# each run adding 60 to 170 KB. This program reads that corpus so an agent does
# not have to
# (`260918-0712_*_how-does-the-curators-edge-survey-know-not-to-re-propose-an-edge-the-user-declined.md`,
# the `Answered:` block's last paragraph).
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT PRINT, AND THAT ABSENCE IS THE RULING. Not the entry's
# consequence group. The suppression key reads the outcome value alone, because
# `not-offered` names the entry the gate never put; a key that read the group
# line beside the outcome line would be the drifting second copy the ruling
# removed. And not the decision: which of the seven values suppresses and which
# re-proposes is authored in `### The suppression read` and nowhere here. This
# program reports and gates nothing — no exit code carries the verdict, the
# stdout-verdict rule every other reporting helper in this directory carries.
#
# THE CORPUS IS EVERY RUN FILE, WITH NOTHING EXCLUDED — not bounded by the
# curator's evidence anchor, not resolved through $SCAN_ANALYSES, and `archive/`
# read like the live tree. A run file records a refusal whatever its age,
# whichever item was claimed when it was written, and whether or not its
# container has since been archived; a bound that drops one drops it silently.
#
# THE OUTCOME PARSE IS A HEURISTIC OVER FREE PROSE and the library header states
# it exactly. Its one property a caller must know: it fails toward `none`, so an
# unrecognised shape costs a re-proposal and never a silent suppression.
#
# Exit codes:
#   0  the read ran. `verdict=` says what it found. `verdict=empty` is a real
#      answer that reaches 0 — no edge was ever proposed in this workbench.
#   1  usage error.
#   2  no fusion workbench above the working directory; nothing to read.
#   3  the plugin's compiled hooks are missing, so nothing could be asked. Its
#      own code and not 2: "no edge was ever proposed here" is an answer about
#      the project, and a broken install must never be reported as one.
#
# The work is hooks/dist/edge-answers.js, resolved relative to this script rather
# than through $FUSION_PLUGIN_ROOT, so an install copy and a work tree each run
# their own build. node is already a hard requirement: the plugin's hooks are
# node programs.

set -euo pipefail

here="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)"
entry="$here/../hooks/dist/edge-answers.js"

if [ ! -f "$entry" ]; then
  echo "fusion-edge-answers: $entry is missing — the plugin's compiled hooks are not installed." >&2
  exit 3
fi

exec node "$entry" "$@"
